ASCII: store oCMOB's signed one-bit flags as -1 like ZenGin.

WriteArchiveAscii::write_bool always wrote 1 for true. ZenGin declares some of
oCMOB's flags as signed one-bit bit-fields, so reading a set flag yields -1 and
the ASCII archiver stores that signed value. A retail ASCII census finds 56
`bool:-1` values among 45,068 boolean entries: 51 `locked` and 5 `moveable`,
with every other observed true boolean written as 1.

The same signed-field rule is already measured more broadly by patch 0017's
BinSafe census: `locked`, `moveable`, and `focusOverride` are the only boolean
entry names that ever hold a non-0/1 value. Mirror that rule here while leaving
ordinary true values and every false value unchanged.

Like 0017, this is not upstreamable as written: selecting field semantics by
archive entry name is a layering violation. The clean upstream form would be
an explicit signed/raw boolean write at the oCMOB save sites.

diff --git a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
--- a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
@@ -354,7 +354,9 @@ namespace zenkit {
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_bool(std::string_view name, bool v) {
-		this->write_entry(name, "bool", v ? "1" : "0");
+		auto const signed_true =
+		    name == "locked" || name == "moveable" || name == "focusOverride";
+		this->write_entry(name, "bool", v ? (signed_true ? "-1" : "1") : "0");
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_color(std::string_view name, Color v) {
